{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MxACBowxz3DhJJt7S84nWm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Changelog\n2\t\n3\tAll notable changes to **ceo-orchestration** are documented in this file.\n4\t\n5\tThe format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),\n6\tand this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).\n7\t\n8\t> **Scope.** This log records *user-visible* changes — new skills, hooks, slash\n9\t> commands, schema/contract changes, and behavior an adopter would notice after\n10\t> installing or upgrading the framework. Internal refactors, test-only churn, and\n11\t> release-engineering bookkeeping are omitted. Counts cited below (as of\n12\t> v1.4.4: 166 skills, 27 slash commands, 201 ADRs, 78 `_lib` modules) are\n13\t> reproducible from the repository via\n14\t> `bash .claude/scripts/local/verify-counts.sh`.\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t## [1.4.4] - 2026-11-03\n19\t\n20\tMaintenance release (PLAN-198). **v1.4.3 never reached npm.** Only\n21\t`v1.4.3-rc.1` was tagged (2026-10-17) and published on GitHub as a\n22\tpre-release. The npm workflow does not publish an rc tag, and no v1.4.3 GA\n23\twas cut, so npm `latest` was still 1.4.2 when this entry was written\n24\t(2026-11-03). The `[1.4.3]` entry below stays as written: it describes the\n25\trc.1 tree only. This entry covers the whole train since v1.4.2: everything\n26\tin `[1.4.3]` is part of this release, together with the changes below.\n27\tComing from v1.4.2, read both entries.\n28\t\n29\tThe requirements do not change from v1.4.2: Claude Code 2.1.280 or later,\n30\tPython 3.9 or later, and Node.js 20 or later for the npm shim. `install.sh`,\n31\t`upgrade.sh` and the settings templates are unchanged since v1.4.3-rc.1;\n32\tthe `--harness codex` helper that `install.sh` sources changed (below).\n33\tWhat changes for an install, on top of `[1.4.3]`:\n34\t\n35\t- A new scan in the Bash guard blocks a destructive command that is reached\n36\t  indirectly, on by default (PLAN-195, ADR-201).\n37\t- Several of the largest hooks and scripts become thin entry points over\n38\t  modules under `.claude/hooks/_lib/` (PLAN-197, ADR-202). A hook you\n39\t  patched by hand must be patched again in its module.\n40\t- A registered hook no longer drains the audit spool at exit (a partial\n41\t  cure).\n42\t- The model checks accept model families, among them other vendors' ids\n43\t  and retired ids, and the Codex pin check accepts a newer Codex CLI on a\n44\t  version floor, without a matching hash (Security and governance, below).\n45\t- The secret redactor that the Codex and Grok egress uses no longer cuts\n46\t  the text at offsets taken from its NFKC form, a defect present since\n47\t  v1.0.0. Its `scan()` offsets now index the text passed in (Fixed,\n48\t  below).\n49\t- The kernel's hard-deny list covers the code that moved out of seven\n50\t  kernel entry points, five flat modules that kernel code calls, and the\n51\t  live-adapter package (Fixed, below).\n52\t- Fixes to backup and restore, to `audit-verify-chain.py\n53\t  --strict-against-counter`, to the harness gate, to the plan-lifecycle\n54\t  hook and to the cost-envelope hook, and three findings of the\n55\t  v1.4.3-rc.1 re-pass.\n56\t\n57\tThe honest part: most of this train after v1.4.3-rc.1 reached `main`\n58\tthrough two pull-request merges, without the signing ceremony. They\n59\tchanged 233 canonical paths, 15 of them kernel paths by the list as\n60\tmerged, and with them the meaning of three governance checks: the VETO"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8251238c-5deb-4f64-879e-480beb36292d","uuid":"06bab3bb-476d-4167-a4cb-715f63fa0a67","timestamp":"2026-11-13T16:42:59.996Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/CHANGELOG.md","content":"# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to **ceo-orchestration** are documented in this file.\n\nThe format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),\nand this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).\n\n> **Scope.** This log records *user-visible* changes — new skills, hooks, slash\n> commands, schema/contract changes, and behavior an adopter would notice after\n> installing or upgrading the framework. Internal refactors, test-only churn, and\n> release-engineering bookkeeping are omitted. Counts cited below (as of\n> v1.4.4: 166 skills, 27 slash commands, 201 ADRs, 78 `_lib` modules) are\n> reproducible from the repository via\n> `bash .claude/scripts/local/verify-counts.sh`.\n\n---\n\n## [1.4.4] - 2026-11-03\n\nMaintenance release (PLAN-198). **v1.4.3 never reached npm.** Only\n`v1.4.3-rc.1` was tagged (2026-10-17) and published on GitHub as a\npre-release. The npm workflow does not publish an rc tag, and no v1.4.3 GA\nwas cut, so npm `latest` was still 1.4.2 when this entry was written\n(2026-11-03). The `[1.4.3]` entry below stays as written: it describes the\nrc.1 tree only. This entry covers the whole train since v1.4.2: everything\nin `[1.4.3]` is part of this release, together with the changes below.\nComing from v1.4.2, read both entries.\n\nThe requirements do not change from v1.4.2: Claude Code 2.1.280 or later,\nPython 3.9 or later, and Node.js 20 or later for the npm shim. `install.sh`,\n`upgrade.sh` and the settings templates are unchanged since v1.4.3-rc.1;\nthe `--harness codex` helper that `install.sh` sources changed (below).\nWhat changes for an install, on top of `[1.4.3]`:\n\n- A new scan in the Bash guard blocks a destructive command that is reached\n  indirectly, on by default (PLAN-195, ADR-201).\n- Several of the largest hooks and scripts become thin entry points over\n  modules under `.claude/hooks/_lib/` (PLAN-197, ADR-202). A hook you\n  patched by hand must be patched again in its module.\n- A registered hook no longer drains the audit spool at exit (a partial\n  cure).\n- The model checks accept model families, among them other vendors' ids\n  and retired ids, and the Codex pin check accepts a newer Codex CLI on a\n  version floor, without a matching hash (Security and governance, below).\n- The secret redactor that the Codex and Grok egress uses no longer cuts\n  the text at offsets taken from its NFKC form, a defect present since\n  v1.0.0. Its `scan()` offsets now index the text passed in (Fixed,\n  below).\n- The kernel's hard-deny list covers the code that moved out of seven\n  kernel entry points, five flat modules that kernel code calls, and the\n  live-adapter package (Fixed, below).\n- Fixes to backup and restore, to `audit-verify-chain.py\n  --strict-against-counter`, to the harness gate, to the plan-lifecycle\n  hook and to the cost-envelope hook, and three findings of the\n  v1.4.3-rc.1 re-pass.\n\nThe honest part: most of this train after v1.4.3-rc.1 reached `main`\nthrough two pull-request merges, without the signing ceremony. They\nchanged 233 canonical paths, 15 of them kernel paths by the list as\nmerged, and with them the meaning of three governance checks: the VETO","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2658}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01MxACBowxz3DhJJt7S84nWm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
